# Paths excluded from knowledge-graph indexing — STEP-004.07.
#
# WHY GENERATED CODE MUST NOT BE INDEXED
#   Two reasons, and the second is the one that matters.
#
#   1. It inflates coverage. `REQ-KG-001` (≥95% files parsed) and `REQ-KG-002`
#      (≥90% symbols owned) are gates on OUR code. Thousands of generated symbols
#      that nobody wrote and nobody can change would make both look healthier
#      while saying nothing about the code a human maintains.
#
#   2. It makes impact analysis wrong in the worst direction. Every generated
#      model would appear as a dependent of every contract type, so a pre-change
#      check on a shared schema would report a blast radius of hundreds — and a
#      number that large is one nobody reads. A gate that always says "high" is a
#      gate that gets skipped.
#
#   The contract documents themselves stay indexed. They are the source, they are
#   hand-written, and a change to one is exactly what a pre-change check should
#   surface.
#
# THESE TWO ENTRIES ARE CURRENTLY REDUNDANT — READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING THEM
#   Measured at 7b1489e: the index is identical with and without them (353 files,
#   5,702 nodes, 7,993 edges). GitNexus already skips paths under a `generated/`
#   directory by default, so these lines remove nothing today.
#
#   The file itself DOES work — a control probe listing `tools/gen_clients.py`
#   removed 16 nodes. So a null result here means "already excluded", not "pattern
#   ignored". If you add a path and see no change, check that before assuming the
#   syntax is wrong.
#
#   They are kept because a default is somebody else's decision: it can change in a
#   minor version, and `src/generated/` can be renamed to something the default does
#   not recognise. Nothing asserts the absence of generated nodes, so either of those
#   would go unnoticed. That gap is carried to STEP-026. See BR-034 §4.

packages/contracts/src/generated/
